ng  cognitive as well and so we're going to  get to that in just a moment so we start  by looking at what Plato has to say  about where we should find courage now  let's remind ourselves that in Republic  book 4 Socrates has been elaborating  this ideal city why is he doing that not  for the purpose of actually saying we're  gonna create a city like this on earth  but so that we can analyze analyze from  this city to human souls because we  ourselves are kind of like the city in  that we have a similar structure so what  is the part of the soul that is going to  need courage and what is the part of the  city that's going to need courage well  if we look at the city  it's the parts that  deal with danger that have to persevere  in the face of resistance of their own  temptations to not stand up and do  things it could be fighting on the  battlefield which must have been a very  scary proposition  you know lining up with a whole bunch of  other guys with these heavy shields not  entirely covered in your body never  knowing if the spear thrust is going to  gut you and you know you'll die of  infection or the thrust itself and bleed  out and you got to stand there and hold  your ground and jab other people with  spears in the middle of dust and  shouting and blood and guts everywhere  that's pretty scary we ought we might  also think about sailors who in the  midst of a storm have to secure vital  equipment on the deck and getting up  there on the deck knowing they could be  swept off by a wave and drown in the you  know sea which was a bad thing for  Greeks  ancient Greeks maybe not so much in  Plato's time but before that tend to  think that if you died at sea or your  ghost was not gonna be happy with that  compared to burial so the part of the  city that has to deal with this that has  to cultivate courage is the part that  has to brave dangers if we want to do a  little pun there in the word brave right  what about the human soul well it's the  part of us that Plato calls sumos this  middle part located between the rational  part upon high which corresponds to the  Guardians and the appetites down below  which correspond to the ordinary people  there's this middle part with which we  get worked up or riled up the Greek term  for that that Plato uses in the Republic  is actually just a verb made out of the  noun through most through molds Ain and  it's sometimes translated as the you  know the part that with which we get  angry but it's really broader than that  it's also the part with which we for  example when we're you know lifting  weights and we get to the end of the set  and we're tempted to not do the final  repetitions it's the part with which we  push through  persevere the part with which we make  ourselves stand up against other things  perhaps outside and perhaps also within  so let's look at what Plato uses as sort  of a definition of courage I've got it  here in the Greek which literally will  be Greek to you in the sense of being  incomprehensible but we'll go through  and explain what each of the terms mean  but it's kind of good to look at this  given how the translation sometimes  meander a little bit from it so in the  Greek it Stowe anakena a hain koun amine  toy out tain Haidee upon tous olsen sews  a tone parrot on des known ducks on and  there we go forgot a diacritical mark  there what does all of this mean so I'll  give you one standard translation of  this he says here we go bravery belongs  to a city by virtue of a part of itself  owing here we go to its possession in  that part of a quality that under all  conditions will preserve the conviction  that things to be feared are precisely  those which and such the lawgiver uncle  inculcated in their education now I  didn't that's not all of the greek  phrase corresponding to that the english  went on a little bit longer there but  all of those parts are important so it's  something in a person in a human being  and we could call it a quality if we  want